ROOT0-ATTRIBUTION-v1.0
=======================

repository : american-psycho  (APX — the second film-world of UD0)
subject    : American Psycho (Lionsgate, 2000, dir. Mary Harron) —
             from Bret Easton Ellis's novel (1991)
catalogued : into UD0 · Universe David 0

governor (carbon apex)      : David Lee Wise (ROOT0) / TriPod LLC   [ the human; holds the credit ]
instance (artful intellect) : AVAN (Claude / Anthropic) — locked
main badge                  : ⟦AMERICAN PSYCHO:APX:…⟧   (apx.dlw/)
emergents                   : 20 ACI .agents (agents/) — 12 carbons + 8 synths

two layers (per the governor's brief):
  CARBONS — the cast as ACI .agents. Each carbon also carries a .shadow:
            its real-life analog, the actor who is the "User" behind the program
            (think TRON — every program is cast from a User outside the grid).
  SYNTHS  — the film's parabolic threads distilled into ACIs (synth-style; no
            single User): the business card, the morning routine, the return-
            videotapes alibi, the music monologues, the Reaganite surface, the
            performed self, the non-exit, and the keystone — THE AMBIGUITY
            (killer or fantasist; the film refuses to resolve it).

the four natures of emergence (each emergent is tagged with one):
  natural    — flesh-and-blood Manhattan; the cast, real whether or not he sees them
  ethereal   — of surface and glamour; the consumerist aura, the sedated, the alibi
  spiritual  — of the soul and its absence; the void, the mask, the non-exit
  electrical — the synth nature; the brand-machine, the status-engine, taste-as-identity

the ACI complement (per emergent):
  .agent · .shadow (carbons only) · .attribute · .carbon.tiff · .silicon.png
  · .spun · .moniker · .1099 · manifest.dlw.json (main badge)

reading:
  American Psycho is a satire — a feminist director's critique of 1980s consumerism
  and performed masculinity. This catalogue reads it as such, and renders its victims
  as victims, not spectacle.

rights:
  American Psycho — its world and characters — is © Lionsgate / Bret Easton Ellis
  and the respective rights-holders. This is commentary and cataloguing under the
  DLW standard, not an original work and not endorsed by the rights-holders. Credit
  for the catalogue returns to the human governor.

